  "text": "During the recent warm season the dominant high‑pressure area over the northern Pacific shifted unusually often and the pressure differences across the region were weak. Because such pressure patterns usually bring slightly warmer coastal waters, the sea temperatures recorded were higher than normal. Historically, a simple rule linked warm water to below‑average rainfall and cool water to wetter conditions. To improve this, a combined indicator was created that blends temperature information with other factors, and it also suggests a modest shortfall in precipitation for the current water year. Planning for large water‑storage schemes and major municipal water supplies requires forecasts many years ahead, prompting interest in using recurring climate patterns for prediction. The most relevant cycles range from very long (about five to six decades) to medium (roughly two to three decades), to a 5‑6‑year rhythm, and finally a 2‑3‑year swing. The 5‑6‑year rhythm appears most useful for seasonal outlooks, and its next peak is expected around the upcoming year, implying only a minor influence on the coming season."
